$, as a residue modulo 13.  (That is, find the value of $n$ that satisfies the congruence such that $0\\le n \\le 12$.)","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"15a7438467508424565479ce3add38ea6193ceeaee18e1f25a33df9c32bd0b10"},"seconds":14.699346780776978,"seed":409172637244960115,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the congruence \\(6n \\equiv 7 \\pmod{13}\\), we need to find the multiplicative inverse of 6 modulo 13. The multiplicative inverse of 6 modulo 13 is a number \\(x\\) such that \\(6x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{13}\\). Once we find this inverse, we can multiply both sides of the original congruence by \\(x\\) to isolate \\(n\\).\n\nFirst, we use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find the inverse of 6 modulo 13. We need to find integers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) such that \\(6x + 13y = 1\\).\n\nPerforming the Euclidean algorithm:\n\\[\n13 = 2 \\cdot 6 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n6 = 6 \\cdot 1 + 0\n\\]\nFrom the first equation, we can express 1 as:\n\\[\n1 = 13 - 2 \\cdot 6\n\\]\nThis shows that \\(x = -2\\) and \\(y = 1\\) is a solution to the equation \\(6x + 13y = 1\\). Therefore, the inverse of 6 modulo 13 is \\(-2\\). Since we want a positive residue, we convert \\(-2\\) to a positive residue modulo 13:\n\\[\n-2 \\equiv 11 \\pmod{13}\n\\]\nSo, the inverse of 6 modulo 13 is 11. Now, we can multiply both sides of the original congruence \\(6n \\equiv 7 \\pmod{13}\\) by 11:\n\\[\n11 \\cdot 6n \\equiv 11 \\cdot 7 \\pmod{13}\n\\]\n\\[\n66n \\equiv 77 \\pmod{13}\n\\]\nSince \\(66 \\equiv 66 - 5 \\cdot 13 = 66 - 65 = 1 \\pmod{13}\\), we have:\n\\[\nn \\equiv 77 \\pmod{13}\n\\]\nNext, we reduce 77 modulo 13:\n\\[\n77 = 5 \\cdot 13 + 12\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\n77 \\equiv 12 \\pmod{13}\n\\]\nTherefore, the solution to the congruence \\(6n \\equiv 7 \\pmod{13}\\) is:\n\\[\nn \\equiv 12 \\pmod{13}\n\\]\nThe value of \\(n\\) that satisfies the congruence such that \\(0 \\leq n \\leq 12\\) is \\(\\boxed{12}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{12}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:387"}
